<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-pay-attention">
  <title>What affects interpretation?</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        "As in most cases when the truth becomes clear you wonder how you could ever have seen things differently."
      </p>
      <p>--- Ruth Rendell, <em>Not in the Flesh</em></p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Interpreting graphics depends on paying attention to the graphics, understanding what is seen, and checking what is seen.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-attention">
    <title>Attention</title>

    <blockquote>
      <p>"Can you describe her?"</p>
      <p>"It's a bit difficult. She was sort of middle-aged and ordinary."</p>
      <p>"What was she wearing?"</p>
      <p>
        "Tweeds, oatmeal flecked with brown, a three-quarter coat with patch pockets... a scarf, felt hat, brown
        shoes, a tussle shirt... and a small blue handkerchief in her breast pocket. I can't remember any more."
      </p>
      <p>"You couldn't have been paying attention."</p>
      <p>--- <em>The Lady Vanishes</em></p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Graphics texts generally consider perception and interpretation, including optical illusions, but do not pay as
      much attention to attention. There is an implicit assumption that viewers who look at a graphic are giving it
      their full attention. That seems as unrealistic as assuming all students attending a lecture are giving it their
      undivided attention.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-bibi1">
      <image source="images/bibi1-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Scatterplot of Biacromial diameter against pelvic breadth (Biiliac) for 507 adults.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Does this display demand attention? Someone might glance at it and decide it does not look interesting. Perhaps
      the measurements do not mean anything to them, or perhaps they do, but the topic does not attract them. They
      might be distracted by coffee, email, noise, fatigue, or boredom. Or they might devote sustained and
      concentrated attention to it. Graphics are often discussed objectively, yet there are many subjective factors
      affecting how they are viewed.
    </p>

    <p>
      Biacromial diameter is a technical term for shoulder width, and Biiliac may be described as pelvic breadth.
      Knowing that might make someone look more closely and wonder that the two measurements are not more correlated.
      Many body measurements are. A few Biiliac values appear lower than the rest. It is curious that the highest
      pelvic breadth value and two of the three lowest have about the same middling shoulder width.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-bibi2">
      <image source="images/bibi2-1.png"/>
      <caption><p>Scatterplot of shoulder width against pelvic breadth for men and women, coloured by sex.</p></caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Generally, women have about the same pelvic breadth as men but narrower shoulder widths. These displays use data
      collected around twenty years ago (@heinz:2003). The authors' proviso should be remembered: the sample is not a
      representative sample from a well-defined population.
    </p>

    <p>
      If information is to be gained from a graphic, it has to be paid some attention. How much attention is paid and
      how that attention is paid are important. A quick scan can suggest graphic type and broad structure. More focused
      attention may be needed to confirm first impressions, identify additional features, and decide what further
      information is useful.
    </p>

    <p>
      Inspecting a graphic once is rarely sufficient. The source should be checked and associated text reviewed.
      Sometimes a graphic should be compared with related graphics; sometimes other questions should be investigated
      before returning to the graphic itself. If a graphic is worth drawing, it should be worth careful and thorough
      study.
    </p>

    <p>
      Initial impressions can be deceptive. Rather than fixating on one visible feature, it is more effective to
      consciously look for others. Like brainstorming, it helps to generate several ideas before being too critical of
      any of them.
    </p>

    <p>
      Expectations influence what is seen, either negatively or positively. If expectations are strong, viewers may
      only seek corroborating evidence. If there are no expectations, viewers may simply accept what they see and lose
      capacity for surprise. It helps to think in advance about what is likely to appear, then look more closely even
      if expectations are met.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-attention-types">
      <title>Types of attention</title>
      <p>
        Before paying full attention, there is preattentive processing (although attention still plays a role,
        @healey:2012). People can pick out two red dots in a cloud of blue dots without trying. They might then use
        focused attention on those points and their neighbourhoods or distribute attention over the whole display.
      </p>
      <p>
        Something may catch a viewer's eye through peripheral vision. @potter:2014 concludes that in some
        circumstances people can see meaning in a photograph within 13 ms. Even when graphics are studied more
        closely, there is Barbara Tversky's fourth law of cognition (@tversky:2019): the mind can override
        perception. These effects help explain why graphics are often poorly interpreted.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-btwmapex">
        <image source="images/btwMapEX-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Winners of Erststimmen seats by political party in Germany 2021.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        What attention is paid to may be goal-driven, stimulus-driven, or history-based. In this map, main party
        regions stand out for goal-driven viewing; smaller urban successes and legend implications support
        stimulus-driven viewing; and prior knowledge reminds us that political maps display area rather than population.
      </p>

      <p>
        @theeuwes:2018 argues that stimulus-driven and history-based selection are effortless while goal-driven
        selection requires effort. It is easier to respond to a strong feature or familiar pattern than to identify a
        feature that may or may not serve a particular goal.
      </p>

      <p>
        Studying a display closely may reveal several features, yet later inspection may reveal others. It is useful to
        revisit graphics afresh, changing size, aspect ratio, position, colour, symbols, and scales. The essential
        principle is to regard graphics as doorways to information, not static pictures.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-attention-cognitive-load">
      <title>Cognitive load</title>
      <p>
        Complex graphics can be difficult to study and understand. Sometimes this is because space is limited;
        sometimes because data are genuinely complex. Anything that increases cognitive load should be avoided.
      </p>

      <p>
        Having graphics and accompanying text on different pages demands extra effort. Poor labelling and awkward
        scales make work. A poorly ordered legend does not help. Overlapping text and confusing colours require
        disentangling. Comparisons become harder when scales that should match do not, or when graphics are not aligned
        as they should be.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-palpcp2x">
        <image source="images/Palpcp2X-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Reordered parallel coordinate plot of Palmer penguin data.</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-attention-rewards">
      <title>Rewards for paying attention</title>
      <p>
        Paying close attention to graphics can reveal much more information. Accepting a title or accompanying text may
        not tell the full story. Graphics should be studied in context, and a closer look at underlying data is often
        warranted.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gapmovie">
        <image source="images/gapMovie-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Gapminder life expectancy data (left) and movie ratings (right).</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-understanding">
    <title>Understanding</title>

    <blockquote>
      <p>"The more I practise, the luckier I get."</p>
      <p>--- Gary Player</p>
    </blockquote>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-understanding-knowledge">
      <title>Knowledge</title>
      <p>
        Knowledge of what graphic forms represent is necessary to understand what features might convey. Individual
        graphics have a general structure with local details. Combined with other graphics they provide an overall
        picture whose interpretation depends on context and background.
      </p>

      <p>
        Background knowledge is indispensable, whether your own or that of collaborating domain experts. With data by
        country over time, knowing how countries have changed matters. With swimming, knowledge of rules and equipment
        changes matters.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-relay400x">
        <image source="images/relay400X-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Best times for 400 m freestyle events for men and women, individual and relay.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The web can help with context, but not always. Going back to original documentation can be valuable. Dataset
        owners and maintainers are often very helpful.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-understanding-experience">
      <title>Experience</title>
      <p>
        Experience changes what people notice. Motives, interest, background knowledge, education, and experience all
        influence interpretation. It helps to study how to look at graphics and to look at many graphics. Just looking
        is not enough.
      </p>

      <p>
        Expertise research suggests that people with more experience recognise meaningful structure better. The same
        likely applies to graphics: familiarity with forms and interpretation practices makes it easier to understand a
        new dataset.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-comtimesx">
        <image source="images/comTimesX-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Finishing times in the 2019 Comrades race for females and males.</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-understanding-biases">
      <title>Possible cognitive biases</title>
      <p>
        Even if proper attention is paid, cognitive biases may hinder proper use of what is seen.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><p>Confirmation bias: look for information supporting prior beliefs.</p></li>
        <li><p>Hindsight bias: past events seem more likely than they were.</p></li>
        <li><p>Recency bias: recent events are weighted more than older ones.</p></li>
        <li><p>Anchoring bias: overemphasis on a reference point, often what is seen first.</p></li>
        <li><p>Availability bias: rely too much on examples to hand.</p></li>
        <li><p>Representativeness bias: ignore base rates and sample size.</p></li>
        <li><p>Perceptual bias: we see what we want to see.</p></li>
      </ul>

      <figure xml:id="fig-olyamdx">
        <image source="images/olyAmdx-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Athletics field events for men: percentage differences in gold performances compared with recent Games.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-btw23x">
        <image source="images/btw23X-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>UpAndDown plot of absolute and relative changes in party support between two German elections.</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-checking">
    <title>Checking</title>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        "Aristotle maintained that women have fewer teeth than men ... it never occurred to him to verify this
        statement by examining his wives' mouths."
      </p>
      <p>--- Bertrand Russell</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      Published graphics may appear polished, attractive, and convincing. Scepticism and thorough checking are called
      for. It is often better to search for evidence that could refute impressions than to look only for support.
      Several independent ways of checking features detected in graphics should be used.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-checking-provenance">
      <title>Provenance</title>
      <p>
        Are data from a reliable source? Is it known how they were collected, edited, and summarised? Are they precise
        measurements or uncertain estimates? Do variables mean what you think they do?
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-checking-looking">
      <title>Looking at the numbers</title>
      <p>
        Do scales look sensible? Perhaps minima or maxima look too extreme. Do data appear complete? Perhaps there are
        gaps in time series or missing categories. Categories missing altogether can be difficult to spot. Sometimes
        there is heaping at particular values due to rounding, sometimes particular values are never recorded, and
        sometimes all data are rounded so a continuous measurement appears discrete.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-checking-varying-plots">
      <title>Varying plots</title>
      <p>
        Graphics redrawn in different ways leave different impressions, emphasising different information. Size, aspect
        ratio, and position can strongly influence what can be seen. Graphics can be resized, moved, and zoomed in or
        out. Plot options can be varied: maxima and minima of scales, origin location, histogram bin widths, point
        sizes, colours, and more. Whether one variation is "better" than another is less important than checking
        several.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-movhists">
        <image source="images/movHists-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Histograms of movie runtimes, the default on the left and a varied one on the right.</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-checking-varying-forms">
      <title>Varying plot forms</title>
      <p>
        Drawing different kinds of plots of the same variable(s) can provide new insights: boxplots or density
        estimates instead of histograms, scatterplots by group instead of a single scatterplot, or a doubledecker plot
        instead of stacked barcharts.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-checking-statistical">
      <title>Statistical checks</title>
      <p>
        Statistical models and graphics complement one another. There are often graphical ways to assess model results,
        but not always statistical models for ideas suggested by graphics. If such models exist, they should be used.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-chesssocsm-1">
        <image source="images/ChessSocSm-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Smooth of chess ratings.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-chesssocsm-2">
        <image source="images/ChessSocSm-2.png"/>
        <caption><p>Smooth of football-league draw proportions.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        If a spineplot suggests non-independence, a chi-squared test could be used. More complicated data may need
        logistic regression or other modelling approaches.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-checking-consequences">
      <title>Checking consequences</title>
      <p>
        Internal checking asks whether patterns found across the whole dataset also occur in subgroups. External
        checking compares with related datasets where relevant.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-histelox">
        <image source="images/histEloX-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Ratings of active and inactive chess players in 2020.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-btwdotvx">
        <image source="../../pngImages/btwDotV-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Seat-winning percentages by party in Germany 2021 and UK 2019.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-gaybx">
        <image source="images/gayBX-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Support and opposition plots for same-sex marriage questions by age and sex.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ubhx">
        <image source="images/ubHX-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Extra seats in German elections since 1949.</p></caption>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-swim50200x">
        <image source="images/swim50200X-1.png"/>
        <caption><p>Best times in seconds for four 50 m events and four 200 m events by sex.</p></caption>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-checking-context">
      <title>Context</title>
      <p>
        Results have to make sense and be interpreted in context. A point pattern in a scatterplot may be judged very
        differently for different datasets or variables. Weekly reporting patterns, definition changes, and collection
        conditions can all affect what is seen.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-checking-discussion">
      <title>Discussing with others, especially domain experts</title>
      <p>
        What one person sees in a graphic may not be what another person sees. Combining independent opinions is more
        effective than relying on one person. Graphics analysts will not have full subject knowledge and should consult
        domain experts.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-checking-replicate">
      <title>Replicate</title>
      <p>
        The ideal is independent replication. Full replication may be impractical for time, organisational, and
        financial reasons, but it remains the target. Partial replication can be achieved by splitting large datasets
        into independent groups or by checking consistency over time in regularly updated datasets.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-checking-example">
      <title>Example of checking</title>
      <p>
        In the swimming examples, initial visual impressions were checked across strokes and distances, alternative
        displays were drawn, and source records were inspected. This uncovered both substantive explanations and a
        classification error.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-pay-attention-main-points">
      <title>Main points</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Attention varies with goals and conditions; cognitive load should be reduced where possible.</p></li>
        <li><p>Understanding depends on graphic literacy, domain knowledge, and experience.</p></li>
        <li><p>Cognitive biases can distort interpretation and must be actively countered.</p></li>
        <li><p>Checking should combine multiple independent strategies, not just one.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
